+@var{codec_name} is only available in @command{ffmpeg}, not in
+@command{ffprobe} and @command{ffplay}. If it is present, it must be the
+canonical name of a codec of this type (as printed by the @option{-codecs}
+option), such as @code{aac} or @code{h264}, and matches streams which have
+both the specified type and codec. For output streams mapped from an input
+stream the codec name refers to the input codec, also when the stream is
+reencoded. Output streams fed by a complex filtergraph have no input codec and
+are never matched by a codec name.
+
+If @var{additional_stream_specifier} is used, then it matches streams which
+have this type and codec, when specified, and also match the
 @var{additional_stream_specifier}. Otherwise, it matches all streams of the
-specified type.
+specified type and codec. For example, @code{a:aac} matches all AAC audio
+streams and @code{a:aac:1} matches the second AAC audio stream.
+
+In @command{ffmpeg}, a token following @var{stream_type} is only taken as
+@var{codec_name} when it is the name of a codec of that type; anything else is
+interpreted as an @var{additional_stream_specifier}, so that forms such as
+@code{a:1} or @code{a:m:language:eng} keep their meaning. Codec names starting
+with a digit (e.g. @code{4xm}) cannot be used, as a digit always starts a
+stream index.
